The retry-increment update inside the catch block is awaited without any guard. If the error being handled came from the database (e.g., the update at lines 169-177 or 179-186 failed) or the row no longer exists (concurrently deleted by another run's deleteMany), this update throws inside the catch — an unhandled exception that aborts the loop, skips every remaining reminder, and fails the request. Wrap the retry update in its own try/catch (or fire-and-forget with .catch()) so failure-handling cannot itself fail:

```ts
} catch (error) {
  await prisma.workflowReminder
    .update({ where: { id: reminder.id }, data: { retryCount: { increment: 1 } } })
    .catch(() => null);
  console.log(`Error scheduling SMS with error ${error}`);
}
```

This assertion can never fail: locator.isChecked() returns a Promise, and expect(promise).toBeTruthy() is a synchronous Jest-style assertion on the Promise object, which is always truthy. With this PR's new DisplayBackupCodes step, onEnable() is deferred until the backup-codes close button is clicked, so the switch is genuinely unchecked here and the test still passes. Close the backup-codes dialog first and use Playwright's async matcher, e.g.:

await page.getByTestId("backup-codes-close").click();
await expect(page.locator(`[data-testid=two-factor-switch]`)).toBeChecked();

or assert the checked state as the second test does: await expect(page.locator(`[data-testid=two-factor-switch][data-state="checked"]`)).toBeVisible();

This verifies only that a toast appears, but the implementation calls showToast('backup_codes_copied') unconditionally (synchronously, without awaiting navigator.clipboard.writeText), so the test passes even if the clipboard write fails or copies empty/wrong content. Grant clipboard permissions in the test context and assert the actual clipboard contents match the displayed codes, e.g.:

await context.grantPermissions(["clipboard-read", "clipboard-write"]);
await page.getByTestId("backup-codes-copy").click();
const clipboardText = await page.evaluate(() => navigator.clipboard.readText());
expect(clipboardText.split("\n")).toHaveLength(10);
expect(clipboardText).toMatch(/^[a-f0-9]{5}-[a-f0-9]{5}(\n[a-f0-9]{5}-[a-f0-9]{5}){9}$/);

The new sender check assumes `integration.metadata["sender"]["login"]` always exists, but `build_integration` only writes `metadata["sender"]` when `state.get("sender")` is truthy (line 376). This branch is reachable with a pre-existing ACTIVE Integration row that has no OrganizationIntegration (e.g., integration previously removed from the org, or rows created before sender was recorded). For such rows `integration.metadata["sender"]` raises KeyError, producing a 500 instead of the intended graceful "Invalid installation request" page. Guard it the same way the OAuth payload is guarded a few lines earlier, e.g.:

```python
sender = integration.metadata.get("sender") or {}
if pipeline.fetch_state("github_authenticated_user") != sender.get("login"):
    return error(request, self.active_organization)
```

This keeps the check fail-closed (missing sender still rejects) while avoiding the unhandled exception.
